<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Political Committee of National Liberation</h1><p class="st-meta">1944 CE – 1944 CE · capital Viniani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The 'Mountain Government' created by the Greek resistance in 1944, which held the first Greek elections in which women voted.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By 1944 the left-wing resistance movement <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-greece">EAM</a> and its army <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-peoples-liberation-army">ELAS</a> controlled much of mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. On 10 March 1944 EAM created the Political Committee of National Liberation (PEEA) at Viniani in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evrytania">Evrytania</a>, as a rival to both the collaborationist <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hellenic-state-1941-1944">Hellenic State</a> and the government-in-exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>PEEA was led first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evripidis-bakirtzis">Colonel Evripidis Bakirtzis</a> and then by the law professor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandros-svolos">Alexandros Svolos</a>. It organised local self-government, people's courts and schools in liberated areas, and in April 1944 held elections for a National Council, which met at Koryschades; women voted for the first time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The Greek forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> mutinied in its support.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lebanese-republic">Lebanon</a> Conference in May 1944, PEEA agreed to join a government of national unity under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-papandreou">Georgios Papandreou</a>, and its ministers entered that government in September. It dissolved after liberation in November 1944, shortly before fighting in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> began.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/political-committee-of-national-liberation.jpg" alt="Political Committee of National Liberation" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: (of code) Makaristos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Greece_(1822-1978).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1944 CE – 1944 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Viniani</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Evripidis Bakirtzis, Alexandros Svolos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>PEEA, Mountain Government</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=political-committee-of-national-liberation" aria-label="Political Committee of National Liberation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5217 780 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Political Committee of National Liberation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5537 1036L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5537 1036h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5537 1036h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Viniani. <a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=political-committee-of-national-liberation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
